1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:09,400
Sally Brown, who was born in Vermont in the early 1800s, had a typically varied schedule for a working woman of the time.
萨莉·布朗19世纪初出生于佛蒙特州，她的日程安排五花八门，这在当时的劳动妇女中颇为典型。

2
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:17,680
As her diary shows, one day she is finishing stockings; another she is milking a cow; another she is refining wool.
从她的日记可以看出，她这一天在织完长筒袜，那一天在挤牛奶，另一天又在精梳羊毛。

3
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,420
All of her jobs were done from home.
她所有的活儿都是在家里干的。

4
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:29,760
The shift from offices to kitchen tables among white-collar workers in 2020 seems unprecedented, and only possible with the Internet.
2020年，白领从办公室转到厨房餐桌前办公，这种转变似乎前所未有，而且只有借助互联网才能实现。

5
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:31,810
But it is nothing new.
但这根本不是什么新鲜事。

6
00:00:32,610 --> 00:00:37,060
Indeed, the history of home-working suggests some surprising parallels with today.
事实上，居家工作的历史显示，过去与今天之间存在一些出人意料的相似之处。

7
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:44,130
It is not easy to put exact numbers on how many people have worked from home during different historical periods.
不同历史时期究竟有多少人在家工作，很难给出确切的数字。

8
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:54,520
Even in Britain, where economic data reach farther back than in any other country, little reliable labour-force data exist until the mid-1800s.
即便是英国——其经济数据的追溯年代比任何其他国家都要久远——在19世纪中叶以前也几乎没有可靠的劳动力数据。

9
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:57,910
Other sources left clues, however.
不过，其他资料留下了一些线索。

10
00:00:58,710 --> 00:01:00,890
One relates to the meaning of the word “house”.
其中一条线索与“house”一词的含义有关。

11
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:03,510
Today it connotes domesticity.
如今，这个词意味着家庭生活。

12
00:01:04,310 --> 00:01:12,120
But up until the 19th century it had a much broader definition, with the suffix “-house” encompassing economic production, too.
但直到19世纪，它的含义都要宽泛得多，后缀“-house”也涵盖经济生产。

13
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:16,290
In “A Christmas Carol”, Scrooge works in a “counting-house”.
在《圣诞颂歌》中，斯克鲁奇在一家“账房”里工作。

14
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:18,980
Architecture offers other hints.
建筑也提供了其他线索。

15
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:29,860
In Britain, many 18th-century houses still have unusually large upstairs windows; cloth-weavers, who worked there, needed as much light as they could get.
在英国，许多18世纪的房屋至今仍有异常大的楼上窗户；在那里工作的织布工需要尽可能多的光线。

16
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:38,010
Around 1900 French administrators took the lead in asking people about their place of work, not only what they did.
1900年前后，法国的行政官员率先询问人们在哪里工作，而不仅仅是从事什么工作。

17
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:43,080
They found that one-third of France’s manufacturing workforce worked from home.
他们发现，法国制造业劳动力中有三分之一在家工作。

18
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:49,960
Danish surveys around the same time found that a tenth of the total workforce did so full-time at home.
大约同一时期，丹麦的调查发现，全部劳动力中有十分之一全职在家工作。

19
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:59,900
These research efforts took place at the high point of the factory-based system of production; in previous decades the share of home-working would have been far higher.
这些调查研究开展之时，正值以工厂为基础的生产制度的鼎盛时期；而在此前的几十年里，居家工作的比例想必还要高得多。

20
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:10,570
According to one estimate for North America, using official data, in the early 1800s more than 40% of the total workforce laboured from home.
根据一项利用官方数据对北美所作的估算，19世纪初，北美全部劳动力中有40%以上在家劳作。

21
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:16,840
Only by 1914 did the majority of the labour force work in an office or factory.
直到1914年，大多数劳动力才在办公室或工厂里工作。

22
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:21,770
The emergence of an at-home industrial workforce had two main causes.
居家工业劳动力的出现有两个主要原因。

23
00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:32,450
The growth of global trade and the rise in per-person income from the 1600s onwards raised demand for manufactured goods such as woolens and watches.
17世纪以来，全球贸易的增长和人均收入的提高，增加了人们对毛织品、钟表等制成品的需求。

24
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:38,760
But the emerging new technology was more suited to small-scale working than large-scale factories.
但当时新兴的技术更适合小规模作业，而不是大规模的工厂。

25
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:41,850
What emerged was called the “putting-out system”.
由此出现的就是所谓的“外包制”。

26
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:48,160
Workers would collect raw materials, and sometimes equipment, from a central depot.
工人们会从一个中心仓库领取原材料，有时还有设备。

27
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:55,270
They would return home and make the goods for a few days, before giving back the finished articles and getting paid.
他们会回到家中制作几天货物，然后交回成品，领取报酬。

28
00:02:56,070 --> 00:03:04,870
Workers were independent contractors: they were paid by the piece, not by the hour, and they had little if any guarantee of work week to week.
工人是独立承包人：他们按件计酬，而不是按小时计酬，而且每周能否有活干，几乎没有什么保障。

29
00:03:05,670 --> 00:03:12,200
Accounts of what it was actually like to work from home in the 18th and 19th centuries are few and far between.
关于18、19世纪在家工作实际上是什么样子，相关记述寥寥无几。

30
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,830
Many putting-out workers were women, who were less likely to write autobiographies.
许多外包工人是女性，而女性写自传的可能性更小。

31
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:21,910
Some characteristics nonetheless emerge from the archives.
尽管如此，从档案中还是能看出一些特点。

32
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:31,960
Unlike today, when most people have one job, people moved from one task to another, depending on where money could be made, like Sally Brown.
与如今大多数人只有一份工作不同，当时的人们会像萨莉·布朗那样，哪里能挣钱，就从一项活计转到另一项活计。

33
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:38,670
Some economic historians suggest that workers were mercilessly exploited under the putting-out system.
一些经济史学家认为，在外包制下，工人受到了残酷的剥削。

34
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:44,910
Those who owned the machines and raw materials enjoyed enormous power over those they employed.
拥有机器和原材料的人对其雇用的人享有巨大的权力。

35
00:03:45,710 --> 00:03:55,280
With workers dispersed across an area, it was difficult for them to team up against exploitative bosses to demand better pay, let alone form trade unions.
由于工人分散在一片区域，他们很难联合起来对抗剥削他们的老板、要求更高的报酬，更不用说组建工会了。

36
00:03:56,080 --> 00:04:07,430
Bosses “could easily gang up against the rural spinner who faced a take-it-or-leave-it offer of work,” argue Jane Humphries and Ben Schneider of Oxford University, in a paper from 2019.
牛津大学的简·汉弗莱斯和本·施耐德在2019年的一篇论文中指出：“老板们可以轻而易举地联手对付乡下的纺纱工，而纺纱工面对的是要么接受、要么拉倒的派活条件。”

37
00:04:08,230 --> 00:04:15,170
As a result, some historians welcome the development of the factory system from the late 18th century onwards.
因此，一些历史学家对18世纪末以来工厂制度的发展表示欢迎。

38
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:24,910
Workers moved from a place where domestic life intermingled freely with economic production to a place solely dedicated to the pursuit of efficiency.
工人们从一个家庭生活与经济生产自由交织的地方，转到了一个专为追求效率而设的地方。

39
00:04:25,710 --> 00:04:35,680
It is hardly surprising that labour productivity was higher in the factory, now that the factory system gradually outperformed the putting-out system and came to replace it.
既然工厂制度逐渐胜过外包制并最终取而代之，工厂里的劳动生产率更高也就不足为奇了。

40
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:45,860
Crammed together in a factory, workers could more easily club together to ask for higher wages; trade unions started to grow from the 1850s onwards.
工人们挤在一家工厂里，就更容易联合起来要求提高工资；从19世纪50年代起，工会开始发展壮大。

41
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:52,090
According to English data, factory workers were paid 10-20% more than home-workers.
根据英国的数据，工厂工人的工资比居家工人高出10%到20%。

42
00:04:52,890 --> 00:04:54,350
But is that the whole story?
但这就是事情的全部吗？

43
00:04:55,150 --> 00:05:08,860
Some home-workers resisted the shift to the factory system—most notably by joining the Luddites, a society of English textile workers in the 19th century who smashed up machines which they perceived were putting them out of a job.
一些居家工人抵制向工厂制度的转变——最突出的方式就是加入卢德派。卢德派是19世纪英国纺织工人组成的团体，他们砸毁了自认为会让自己丢掉饭碗的机器。

44
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:19,360
Another explanation is that factory owners, at least in the short term, had little option but to offer higher wages in order to entice workers from their homes.
另一种解释是，至少在短期内，工厂主别无选择，只能提供更高的工资，以吸引工人离开家。

45
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:23,110
That suggests that home-working had its advantages.
这表明，居家工作自有其好处。

46
00:05:23,910 --> 00:05:30,490
Home-workers in rural or semi-rural areas could forage for fuel and food, and so boost their meagre incomes.
在农村或半农村地区，居家工人可以搜寻燃料和食物，从而补贴他们微薄的收入。

47
00:05:31,290 --> 00:05:45,260
One observer in 1813 noted that women in Surrey, a county close to London, were making three shillings a week from cutting down heath to make brooms—“miserable productions and trifling employments”, in his view.
1813年，一位观察者注意到，在伦敦附近的萨里郡，妇女们靠砍伐石南制作扫帚，每周能挣3先令——在他看来，那是“粗劣的产品，微不足道的营生”。

48
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:50,580
But three shillings a week was not far off average female earnings at the time.
但每周3先令和当时女性的平均收入相差无几。

49
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:54,300
Home-workers also had more control over their time.
居家工人对自己的时间也有更多的掌控。

50
00:05:55,100 --> 00:06:01,800
So long as the work was done to the required standard and on time, they were not told exactly when or how to do it.
只要按要求的标准按时完成工作，就没人确切规定他们什么时候干、怎么干。

51
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:09,920
That was in sharp contrast to the factory, where every aspect of life was planned in advance and workers were closely monitored.
这与工厂形成了鲜明对比：在工厂里，生活的方方面面都是事先安排好的，工人们受到严密监控。

52
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:22,790
And home-workers could decide on the exact mix between work and leisure—in contrast to factory workers, who either worked the 12- or 14-hour days stipulated by the factory owner or none at all.
而且居家工人可以确切决定工作与休闲各占多少——工厂工人则不同，要么按工厂主的规定每天工作12或14个小时，要么一点活儿都没有。

53
00:06:23,590 --> 00:06:28,820
Average working hours in the 18th century were shorter than they became in the 19th.
18世纪的平均工作时间比19世纪的要短。

54
00:06:29,620 --> 00:06:47,200
After drinking heavily on Sunday evening, home-workers often took the day off before they went “reluctantly back to work Tuesday, warmed to the task Wednesday, and laboured furiously Thursday and Friday”, as David Landes, an economic historian at Harvard University, put it.
正如哈佛大学经济史学家戴维·兰德斯所说，周日晚上喝得酩酊大醉之后，居家工人往往会休息一天，然后“周二不情不愿地回去干活，周三进入状态，周四和周五则拼命劳作”。

55
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:58,520
In 1920 Max Weber, a German sociologist, argued that the separation of the worker’s place of work from their home had “extraordinarily far-reaching” consequences.
1920年，德国社会学家马克斯·韦伯提出，工人的工作地点与家分离，产生了“极其深远”的影响。

56
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:09,760
The factory was more efficient than the home-based system which had preceded it—but it was also a space in which workers had less control over their lives, and where they had much less fun.
工厂比在它之前的居家生产制度更有效率——但在这个空间里，工人对自己生活的掌控也更少了，乐趣也少得多。

57
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:16,950
Depending on how permanent it proves to be, today’s shift back to the home could have similarly far-reaching effects.
如今重回家中工作的转变，视其持久程度而定，可能会产生同样深远的影响。
